findings.body:
The new branch is supposed to skip only the theme reset, but `return` exits the whole `tearDown`:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="27-37"}

On iOS 17+, that means:
- **Every** `OnboardingTests` test on iPad skips `app.terminate()` and `BaseTestCase.tearDown()`, not just `testSelectBottomPlacement`.
- `testSelectBottomPlacement` skips them on iPhone too.

The app stays running into the next test, and any cleanup added to `BaseTestCase.tearDown` later will silently not run for these cases. FullFunctionalTestPlan runs most of `OnboardingTests`, so this applies on iPad CI.

The comment is also misleading. It talks about the toolbar option on iPad, but the condition skips the theme reset for every iPad test, including `testFirstRunTourDarkMode`, which leaves the app in Dark.

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="27-37"}
        let skipThemeReset = name.contains("testSelectBottomPlacement") || iPad()
        if #available(iOS 17.0, *), !skipThemeReset {
            switchThemeToDarkOrLight(theme: "Light")
        }
        app.terminate()
        try await super.tearDown()

Wrapping `XCTSkipIf(true, …)` in `if iPad()` is a roundabout way to write an unconditional skip:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="349-352"}

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="349-352"}
        try XCTSkipIf(iPad(), "Toolbar option not available for iPad")

Moving the skip before the Terms of Service tap is a good change. Only the shape of the check needs simplifying.

The paste-permission alert is animated in after `pasteAction` is tapped. A 0.5s `tapIfExists` window can miss it on slower CI simulators:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/BaseTestCase.swift" lines="470-473"}

If it misses, the next step, `mozWaitForValueContains(urlBar, value: url)`, fails while the alert is still on screen. That's the same flake this line was meant to prevent. `tapIfExists` returns as soon as the element appears, so raising the timeout to 2-3s only costs time when no alert is shown.
